  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure exits to the east through the day today. The majority \nof the forecast area should remain dry, though isolated showers and \nstorms will develop along and east of I-77 as mid to low level \nmoisture increases over an area of isentropic lift. Showers will \nremain isolated with a few weak thunderstorms possible as \ninstability increases to 300-500 J/kg. Patchy fog remains possible \nthrough daybreak today and again overnight tonight into Sunday \nmorning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nUpper level ridge over the western CONUS builds east allowing for an \nupper trough to deepen across the eastern Great Lakes. Multiple \nshortwaves will dive southeast over the region bringing the \npossibility of numerous rounds of showers and thunderstorms Sunday \nthrough Tuesday. Strong to severe thunderstorms remain possible \nSunday and Monday though there remains a large amount of uncertainty \nin the location, coverage, and intensity of thunderstorms. The Storm \nPrediction Center has included the majority of the forecast area in \na Day 2 Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for severe weather Sunday, \nparticularly Sunday afternoon. Primary hazards in any strong to \nsevere storm on Sunday will be damaging wind gusts and large hail. \nOrganized severe thunderstorms remain possible Monday afternoon\nand evening across the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley as a potent \ntrough and strong mid-level jet dive across the region. The \nentire area is in a Slight Risk (Level 2 of 5) for severe \nweather for Monday afternoon and evening. Given strong deep \nlayer shear under the upper level jet, a corridor of damaging \nwind is possible across portions of Northern Ohio. Showers and \nstorms remain possible on Tuesday as the upper level low slowly \nexits to the east. Trending drier by Wednesday and Thursday.",
